1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,338
Welcome back to Code Quest.

2
00:00:02,358 --> 00:00:05,540
This is Chapter 6, the start of Module 2.

3
00:00:05,567 --> 00:00:07,930
Today your programs learn to decide.

4
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:11,307
They check a fact, and then they choose
what to do.

5
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:16,019
And an AI assistant will promise a free
biscuit for exactly 15.

6
00:00:16,039 --> 00:00:17,886
Its own code will say no.

7
00:00:18,487 --> 00:00:21,360
Remember the bill splitter from Chapter 5?

8
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:26,527
Someone typed 0 people, and the program
crashed with a Zero Division Error.

9
00:00:26,567 --> 00:00:29,395
And it could not treat customers
differently.

10
00:00:29,415 --> 00:00:32,497
A member and a visitor got exactly the
same bill.

11
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:36,487
It ran every line, every time, whatever
the answers were.

12
00:00:37,087 --> 00:00:39,040
So here is today's mission.

13
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:42,987
Asha's checkout asks three questions, as
in Chapter 4.

14
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:44,170
Then it decides.

15
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:46,628
Members get a 10 percent discount.

16
00:00:46,648 --> 00:00:49,013
Everyone else pays the full bill.

17
00:00:49,033 --> 00:00:50,451
And it decides again.

18
00:00:50,471 --> 00:00:53,393
Pay 15 or more, and you get a free
biscuit.

19
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:56,374
Two decisions, made by the program itself.

20
00:00:56,394 --> 00:00:57,572
Let's build them.

21
00:00:58,150 --> 00:01:01,640
Write comparisons, the questions a program
can ask.

22
00:01:01,667 --> 00:01:05,867
Write if, elif and else blocks, with
colons and indentation.

23
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:09,150
Combine conditions with and, or, and not.

24
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:14,080
Compare text answers safely, whatever
capital letters the user types.

25
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:16,761
And fix the errors that beginners meet
with if.

26
00:01:17,350 --> 00:01:19,777
Real programs decide all the time.

27
00:01:19,797 --> 00:01:22,296
A cash machine checks your balance.

28
00:01:22,316 --> 00:01:25,390
A shop gives free delivery for a big
order.

29
00:01:25,433 --> 00:01:29,199
A login page lets you in only when the
password matches.

30
00:01:29,219 --> 00:01:32,713
Each one checks a fact, and then chooses
what to do.

31
00:01:33,283 --> 00:01:37,360
A condition is a question that Python
answers with True or False.

32
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,629
You met True and False in Chapter 2.

33
00:01:39,649 --> 00:01:42,213
They are the two values of the type bool.

34
00:01:42,233 --> 00:01:45,873
Double equals asks: are these two values
equal?

35
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:51,180
Exclamation mark equals asks the opposite:
are they different?

36
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:52,941
Less than, and greater than.

37
00:01:52,961 --> 00:01:56,840
Look closely: 15 is not greater than 15,
so that one is False.

38
00:01:56,867 --> 00:02:00,979
Add an equals sign, and the value itself
is included.

39
00:02:00,999 --> 00:02:05,267
15 is greater than or equal to 15, so this
one is True.

40
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:08,793
And remember: one equals sign gives a name
a value.

41
00:02:08,813 --> 00:02:10,997
Two equals signs ask a question.

42
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:14,057
An if statement asks a condition.

43
00:02:14,077 --> 00:02:17,407
If the answer is True, Python runs the
block.

44
00:02:17,427 --> 00:02:19,790
If it is False, Python skips it.

45
00:02:19,833 --> 00:02:25,296
The block is the group of lines that are
indented, four spaces in, under the line

46
00:02:25,316 --> 00:02:26,853
that ends with a colon.

47
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:30,551
The last line is not indented, so it
always runs.

48
00:02:31,153 --> 00:02:33,595
Think of a gate at the cafe door.

49
00:02:33,615 --> 00:02:36,878
It checks one fact: is this person a
member?

50
00:02:36,898 --> 00:02:37,997
True, it opens.

51
00:02:38,017 --> 00:02:39,713
False, it stays closed.

52
00:02:39,733 --> 00:02:43,168
But unlike a person at a door, the gate
cannot guess.

53
00:02:43,188 --> 00:02:47,730
Yes with a capital Y and yes with a small
y are two different answers.

54
00:02:48,353 --> 00:02:49,231
Quick check.

55
00:02:49,251 --> 00:02:50,802
To pay is exactly 15.

56
00:02:50,822 --> 00:02:53,346
Which of these conditions is True?

57
00:03:04,713 --> 00:03:04,854
C.

58
00:03:04,874 --> 00:03:06,858
Greater than or equal to.

59
00:03:06,878 --> 00:03:09,663
The equals sign includes 15 itself.

60
00:03:10,253 --> 00:03:11,311
Take a breather.

61
00:03:11,331 --> 00:03:13,263
Here is what you know so far.

62
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:16,565
Next, Asha asks Python her first
questions.

63
00:03:21,317 --> 00:03:21,826
Step 1.

64
00:03:21,846 --> 00:03:24,168
Asha stores the tea price, 3.5.

65
00:03:24,188 --> 00:03:26,737
Line 3 asks: is it greater than 3?

66
00:03:26,767 --> 00:03:34,037
Line 4 asks if it equals 3.5, and line 5
asks if it is different from 3.5.

67
00:03:34,067 --> 00:03:37,786
Line 6 asks if it is less than or equal to
3.

68
00:03:37,806 --> 00:03:39,947
Line 7 compares two words.

69
00:03:39,967 --> 00:03:43,674
And line 8 asks for the type of a
comparison.

70
00:03:55,047 --> 00:03:56,913
Lines 3 and 4 are True.

71
00:03:56,933 --> 00:04:01,917
Line 5 is False, because the price is not
different from 3.5.

72
00:04:01,967 --> 00:04:03,408
Line 7 is False too.

73
00:04:03,428 --> 00:04:09,107
Tea with a capital T and tea with a small
t are different text, like the gate.

74
00:04:09,133 --> 00:04:12,919
And line 8 shows the type of a comparison:
bool.

75
00:04:13,517 --> 00:04:14,035
Step 2.

76
00:04:14,055 --> 00:04:16,878
Line 2 asks for the number of people.

77
00:04:16,898 --> 00:04:22,027
Line 3 is the if: the condition, people
equals 0, and then a colon.

78
00:04:22,067 --> 00:04:24,224
Lines 4 and 5 are indented.

79
00:04:24,244 --> 00:04:25,757
They are the block.

80
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:27,568
Line 6 is not indented.

81
00:04:27,588 --> 00:04:29,433
It is outside the block.

82
00:04:40,813 --> 00:04:46,033
The condition is True, so both lines of
the block run, and then line 6.

83
00:04:46,067 --> 00:04:49,056
Type 4 instead, and the condition is
False.

84
00:04:49,076 --> 00:04:52,626
Python skips the block, and prints only:
Thank you.

85
00:04:53,217 --> 00:04:53,717
Step 3.

86
00:04:53,737 --> 00:04:57,136
This is the fix for the bill splitter's
crash.

87
00:04:57,156 --> 00:05:01,001
Line 4 asks: is the number of people
greater than 0?

88
00:05:01,021 --> 00:05:03,677
If so, lines 5 and 6 share the bill.

89
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:04,966
Line 7 says else.

90
00:05:04,986 --> 00:05:08,220
Its block runs when the condition is
False.

91
00:05:08,267 --> 00:05:11,695
Exactly one of the two blocks runs, never
both.

92
00:05:11,715 --> 00:05:13,749
Then line 9 runs either way.

93
00:05:25,113 --> 00:05:25,328
No.

94
00:05:25,348 --> 00:05:29,318
0 is not greater than 0, so the condition
is False.

95
00:05:29,338 --> 00:05:31,743
Python jumps to the else block.

96
00:05:31,767 --> 00:05:35,027
The division never happens, so there is no
crash.

97
00:05:35,047 --> 00:05:37,639
A clear message instead, and then Done.

98
00:05:38,217 --> 00:05:38,744
Step 4.

99
00:05:38,764 --> 00:05:40,698
The cafe has two rewards.

100
00:05:40,718 --> 00:05:44,217
A free cake for 50 or more, on lines 3 and
4.

101
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:45,353
Elif means else if.

102
00:05:45,373 --> 00:05:48,207
Lines 5 and 6 give a free biscuit for 15
or more.

103
00:05:48,233 --> 00:05:52,043
And the else block catches everything
else.

104
00:05:52,067 --> 00:05:57,506
Python checks from the top, and runs only
the first block whose condition is True.

105
00:05:57,526 --> 00:05:59,037
Then it skips the rest.

106
00:06:10,413 --> 00:06:11,602
A free biscuit.

107
00:06:11,622 --> 00:06:15,149
Line 3 is False, because 15 is less than
50.

108
00:06:15,169 --> 00:06:19,823
Line 5 is True, because greater than or
equal includes 15.

109
00:06:19,867 --> 00:06:24,702
Exactly 15 is a boundary value: the point
where the result changes.

110
00:06:24,722 --> 00:06:28,543
And notice the order: the biggest amount
comes first.

111
00:06:28,563 --> 00:06:30,210
It matters later today.

112
00:06:30,783 --> 00:06:31,287
Step 5.

113
00:06:31,307 --> 00:06:33,754
Sometimes one fact is not enough.

114
00:06:33,774 --> 00:06:36,893
And is True only when both sides are True.

115
00:06:36,933 --> 00:06:39,713
Or is True when at least one side is True.

116
00:06:39,733 --> 00:06:42,960
And not turns True into False, and False
into True.

117
00:06:43,553 --> 00:06:46,533
Asha is a member, but she pays only 12.

118
00:06:46,567 --> 00:06:49,233
Line 5 joins two facts with and.

119
00:06:49,253 --> 00:06:52,592
Line 6 joins the same two facts with or.

120
00:07:03,947 --> 00:07:05,172
Line 5 is False.

121
00:07:05,192 --> 00:07:09,997
She is a member, but 12 is not 15 or more,
and and needs both.

122
00:07:10,033 --> 00:07:11,208
Line 6 is True.

123
00:07:11,228 --> 00:07:14,313
Or needs only one, and she is a member.

124
00:07:14,333 --> 00:07:16,466
Line 7, not a member, is False.

125
00:07:16,486 --> 00:07:19,661
And line 8 is True, because the day is
Friday.

126
00:07:19,681 --> 00:07:22,439
Each side of or needs a full comparison.

127
00:07:23,053 --> 00:07:23,878
Here is why.

128
00:07:23,898 --> 00:07:26,692
This line forgets the second day equals.

129
00:07:26,712 --> 00:07:32,390
Python checks the word Saturday on its
own, and any text that is not empty counts

130
00:07:32,410 --> 00:07:32,953
as True.

131
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:34,960
So the music plays on a Monday.

132
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:38,154
Always write a full comparison on each
side of or.

133
00:07:38,753 --> 00:07:39,662
Quick check.

134
00:07:39,682 --> 00:07:42,524
Is member is False, and to pay is 20.

135
00:07:42,544 --> 00:07:47,010
What is: is member, or to pay greater than
or equal to 15?

136
00:07:58,380 --> 00:07:58,515
A.

137
00:07:58,535 --> 00:07:58,903
True.

138
00:07:58,923 --> 00:08:05,036
The customer is not a member, but 20 is 15
or more, and or needs only one side.

139
00:08:05,653 --> 00:08:06,170
Breather.

140
00:08:06,190 --> 00:08:08,793
Roll your shoulders, and take a slow
breath.

141
00:08:08,833 --> 00:08:14,272
One more piece, and Asha's checkout is
ready: answers typed by real people.

142
00:08:19,017 --> 00:08:19,487
Step 6.

143
00:08:19,507 --> 00:08:23,763
Real people type Yes, or yes, or yes with
a space at the end.

144
00:08:23,783 --> 00:08:26,357
To the gate, those are all different.

145
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:28,995
So line 3 cleans the answer first.

146
00:08:29,015 --> 00:08:31,918
Strip removes the spaces at both ends.

147
00:08:31,938 --> 00:08:35,610
Lower makes every letter small, as in
Chapter 3.

148
00:08:35,633 --> 00:08:39,055
Only then does it compare with yes, in
small letters.

149
00:08:50,413 --> 00:08:51,571
Yes, it is True.

150
00:08:51,591 --> 00:08:55,179
After strip and lower, the answer is
exactly yes.

151
00:08:55,199 --> 00:08:57,388
So the member discount prints.

152
00:08:57,983 --> 00:08:59,222
Back to the mission.

153
00:08:59,242 --> 00:09:01,173
Lines 2 to 4 ask the questions.

154
00:09:01,193 --> 00:09:05,453
Line 4 cleans the member answer straight
away, with strip and lower.

155
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:09,810
Line 6 works out the bill: the number of
teas times 3.5.

156
00:09:09,833 --> 00:09:12,260
Lines 7 to 10 decide the discount.

157
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:13,915
Members get 10 percent.

158
00:09:13,935 --> 00:09:16,433
Everyone else gets a discount of 0.

159
00:09:16,467 --> 00:09:21,367
Line 11 takes the discount away, and lines
13 to 15 print the bill.

160
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:26,180
Lines 16 to 19 decide the reward: a
biscuit for 15 or more.

161
00:09:37,547 --> 00:09:39,497
Six teas cost 21.00.

162
00:09:39,517 --> 00:09:43,537
She is a member, so the discount is 2.10.

163
00:09:43,567 --> 00:09:44,762
She pays 18.90.

164
00:09:44,782 --> 00:09:48,247
That is 15 or more, so yes: a free
biscuit!

165
00:09:48,267 --> 00:09:49,538
Mission complete!

166
00:09:49,558 --> 00:09:55,846
And the Chapter 5 crash is gone too: the
else from step 3 stops a division by zero.

167
00:09:55,866 --> 00:09:59,797
Now Asha wants to be sure her biscuit rule
is right.

168
00:09:59,817 --> 00:10:03,293
She uses an AI assistant, one stage at a
time.

169
00:10:03,883 --> 00:10:10,187
This chapter's AI skill is asking for edge
cases: unusual inputs that can break a

170
00:10:10,207 --> 00:10:14,403
condition, like exactly 15, zero, or a
capital letter.

171
00:10:14,433 --> 00:10:17,824
Ask the AI to list them, then run every
one.

172
00:10:17,844 --> 00:10:21,622
And the safety habits from Chapter 1 still
apply.

173
00:10:22,220 --> 00:10:23,329
Stage 1: plan.

174
00:10:23,349 --> 00:10:30,022
Asha describes the biscuit rule in words,
and asks for the boundary values to test.

175
00:10:30,042 --> 00:10:30,990
No code yet.

176
00:10:31,033 --> 00:10:35,937
The AI lists 14.99, 15 and 20, with the
result for each.

177
00:10:35,957 --> 00:10:40,420
15 is the boundary value, where the result
changes.

178
00:10:41,020 --> 00:10:42,133
Stage 2: write.

179
00:10:42,153 --> 00:10:47,496
Asha writes the condition herself, and
asks what happens at exactly 15.

180
00:10:47,516 --> 00:10:51,650
The AI explains that greater than or equal
includes 15.

181
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:53,743
Asha runs it with 15.0.

182
00:10:53,763 --> 00:10:55,358
The reward prints.

183
00:10:55,378 --> 00:10:58,856
The explanation and the real run agree.

184
00:10:59,453 --> 00:11:00,865
Stage 3: generate.

185
00:11:00,885 --> 00:11:03,331
Asha gives the AI her own code.

186
00:11:03,351 --> 00:11:09,456
She asks it to add an else, not to change
her condition, and to say what 15.0

187
00:11:09,476 --> 00:11:10,013
prints.

188
00:11:10,033 --> 00:11:14,119
The AI sends back the code, and says: a
free biscuit.

189
00:11:14,139 --> 00:11:16,133
It sounds completely sure.

190
00:11:16,167 --> 00:11:19,368
Compare its line 3 with the line Asha gave
it.

191
00:11:30,747 --> 00:11:30,932
No.

192
00:11:30,952 --> 00:11:35,253
The AI quietly changed greater than or
equal into greater than.

193
00:11:35,273 --> 00:11:37,996
Asha told it not to touch her condition.

194
00:11:38,016 --> 00:11:40,945
She will prove the problem with edge
cases.

195
00:11:41,553 --> 00:11:42,554
Stage 4: test.

196
00:11:42,574 --> 00:11:44,159
This is today's skill.

197
00:11:44,179 --> 00:11:49,483
Asha asks the AI for the edge cases, and
the output each one should give.

198
00:11:49,533 --> 00:11:54,663
The AI lists 14.99, the boundary value 15,
and 20.

199
00:11:54,700 --> 00:11:57,722
She runs all three on the AI's own code.

200
00:11:57,742 --> 00:12:01,600
14.99 and 20 are right, but 15.0 prints no
biscuit.

201
00:12:01,633 --> 00:12:05,068
Now the AI admits it changed her
condition.

202
00:12:05,088 --> 00:12:08,281
She puts back her greater than or equal.

203
00:12:08,887 --> 00:12:13,730
With her own condition back on line 3,
15.0 gets the biscuit.

204
00:12:13,750 --> 00:12:16,760
The prediction and the real run agree.

205
00:12:17,353 --> 00:12:18,341
Stage 5: debug.

206
00:12:18,361 --> 00:12:22,572
Back when Asha first typed the rule, she
wrote one equals sign.

207
00:12:22,592 --> 00:12:26,333
Python stopped with a Syntax Error, before
any line ran.

208
00:12:26,367 --> 00:12:28,529
She asks for the cause first.

209
00:12:28,549 --> 00:12:30,335
The AI points to line 3.

210
00:12:30,355 --> 00:12:35,527
Python suggests double equals, but that
would reward only exactly 15.

211
00:12:35,547 --> 00:12:39,214
The rule is 15 or more, so greater than or
equal.

212
00:12:39,820 --> 00:12:40,997
Stage 6: improve.

213
00:12:41,017 --> 00:12:42,195
The rewards work.

214
00:12:42,215 --> 00:12:47,337
Asha asks for at most two readability
improvements that do not change the

215
00:12:47,357 --> 00:12:47,830
output.

216
00:12:47,867 --> 00:12:50,526
Suggestion 1, a comment, helps a reader.

217
00:12:50,546 --> 00:12:55,817
Suggestion 2 says: check the biscuit rule
first, because it is the most common.

218
00:12:55,867 --> 00:12:58,079
Remember the order from step 4?

219
00:12:58,099 --> 00:13:00,024
Asha does not trust it yet.

220
00:13:00,044 --> 00:13:02,545
She tests it with a big amount, 60.

221
00:13:03,153 --> 00:13:05,303
Here is the suggested order.

222
00:13:05,323 --> 00:13:08,325
The biscuit rule on line 3 comes first.

223
00:13:08,345 --> 00:13:11,191
The cake rule on line 5 comes second.

224
00:13:22,547 --> 00:13:24,601
A free biscuit, not a cake!

225
00:13:24,621 --> 00:13:30,287
60 is 15 or more, so line 3 is True first,
and Python never checks line 5.

226
00:13:30,333 --> 00:13:31,923
The AI was wrong again.

227
00:13:31,943 --> 00:13:37,453
Asha keeps her order, with the biggest
amount first, and adds only the comment.

228
00:13:38,053 --> 00:13:38,842
Quick check.

229
00:13:38,862 --> 00:13:40,391
The rule is 15 or more.

230
00:13:40,411 --> 00:13:45,713
Which test value catches greater than
written instead of greater than or equal?

231
00:13:57,080 --> 00:13:57,214
B.

232
00:13:57,234 --> 00:13:58,980
15, the boundary value.

233
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:03,281
That is the only value where the two
operators disagree.

234
00:14:03,887 --> 00:14:04,494
Breather.

235
00:14:04,514 --> 00:14:10,072
Asha caught two AI mistakes, with two
things you learned today: boundary values,

236
00:14:10,092 --> 00:14:11,397
and the elif order.

237
00:14:11,433 --> 00:14:14,691
Next, the four mistakes beginners make
most with if.

238
00:14:14,711 --> 00:14:16,896
You have already met the first one.

239
00:14:21,617 --> 00:14:26,397
Mistake 1 is the one Asha debugged: one
equals sign in a condition.

240
00:14:26,417 --> 00:14:28,905
The message suggests double equals.

241
00:14:28,925 --> 00:14:32,989
Ignore the other suggestion; this course
does not use it.

242
00:14:33,587 --> 00:14:34,360
Mistake 2.

243
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:38,088
Every if, elif and else line ends with a
colon.

244
00:14:38,108 --> 00:14:43,562
Line 3 has none, and the message says
exactly that: expected a colon.

245
00:14:44,153 --> 00:14:44,938
Now you try.

246
00:14:44,958 --> 00:14:48,826
Remember how Python knows which lines
belong to the block.

247
00:15:00,180 --> 00:15:04,622
Line 4 is not indented, so the if on line
3 has no block.

248
00:15:04,642 --> 00:15:07,440
Python reports an Indentation Error.

249
00:15:07,467 --> 00:15:10,423
The fix: indent line 4 by four spaces.

250
00:15:10,443 --> 00:15:14,183
Most code editors do it for you after the
colon.

251
00:15:14,787 --> 00:15:15,404
One more.

252
00:15:15,424 --> 00:15:19,440
Asha is a member, and the answer stored on
line 2 is Yes.

253
00:15:19,460 --> 00:15:20,715
Think of the gate.

254
00:15:32,080 --> 00:15:36,299
Yes with a capital Y is not equal to yes
with a small y.

255
00:15:36,319 --> 00:15:40,840
No error at all, a wrong result, and a
member pays too much.

256
00:15:40,867 --> 00:15:45,870
The fix is the one from step 6: compare
the answer after strip and lower.

257
00:15:46,487 --> 00:15:47,355
Last breather.

258
00:15:47,375 --> 00:15:50,147
These errors will not surprise you any
more.

259
00:15:50,167 --> 00:15:53,481
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

260
00:15:58,217 --> 00:15:58,618
First.

261
00:15:58,638 --> 00:16:03,109
Asha wants the free biscuit only for
members who pay 15 or more.

262
00:16:03,129 --> 00:16:05,636
Which word joins the two conditions?

263
00:16:17,013 --> 00:16:22,322
Write it, then test it with a member who
pays 12, and a visitor who pays 20.

264
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:23,411
Second.

265
00:16:23,431 --> 00:16:26,186
Some customers type y, instead of yes.

266
00:16:26,206 --> 00:16:29,398
How could one condition accept both
answers?

267
00:16:40,780 --> 00:16:43,721
Predict which answers still fail, then
test them.

268
00:16:44,317 --> 00:16:45,357
Let's recap.

269
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:49,410
A condition works out to True or False,
the two values of bool.

270
00:16:49,433 --> 00:16:50,928
Double equals compares.

271
00:16:50,948 --> 00:16:53,363
One equals sign gives a name a value.

272
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:58,537
If runs its indented block only when the
condition is True, and else runs when it

273
00:16:58,557 --> 00:16:59,110
is False.

274
00:16:59,133 --> 00:17:02,690
Only the first True elif block runs, so
order matters.

275
00:17:02,710 --> 00:17:05,075
And, or, and not combine conditions.

276
00:17:05,095 --> 00:17:07,923
Clean text answers before you compare
them.

277
00:17:07,967 --> 00:17:11,884
And with AI: ask for the edge cases, and
run every one.

278
00:17:12,487 --> 00:17:13,609
One last check.

279
00:17:13,629 --> 00:17:16,580
The cake rule, 50 or more, comes first.

280
00:17:16,600 --> 00:17:19,169
Then the biscuit rule, 15 or more.

281
00:17:19,189 --> 00:17:20,769
To pay is exactly 50.

282
00:17:20,789 --> 00:17:21,683
What prints?

283
00:17:33,047 --> 00:17:33,183
D.

284
00:17:33,203 --> 00:17:34,122
A free cake.

285
00:17:34,142 --> 00:17:40,380
50 is greater than or equal to 50, so the
first block runs, and Python skips the

286
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:40,771
rest.

287
00:17:41,387 --> 00:17:42,710
Mission complete!

288
00:17:42,730 --> 00:17:46,423
You earned 100 XP and the Decision Maker
badge.

289
00:17:48,650 --> 00:17:50,236
Now it is your turn.

290
00:17:50,256 --> 00:17:54,090
The practice file has eight tasks, worth
170 XP.

291
00:17:54,133 --> 00:17:57,433
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

292
00:17:57,467 --> 00:18:02,127
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each,
including the cafe's opening hours.

293
00:18:02,167 --> 00:18:04,797
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

294
00:18:04,817 --> 00:18:09,595
In the last one, an AI's delivery rule
fails at its boundary value.

295
00:18:09,615 --> 00:18:10,097
Fix it.

296
00:18:10,133 --> 00:18:12,890
Test every program with the boundary
value.

297
00:18:12,910 --> 00:18:15,666
Try each task before you open the
solution.

298
00:18:16,250 --> 00:18:19,824
Asha's program now says: people must be 1
or more.

299
00:18:19,844 --> 00:18:21,550
But then it simply ends.

300
00:18:21,570 --> 00:18:23,850
The customer has to start again.

301
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:29,379
In Chapter 7, you will use while loops to
ask the question again, and again, until

302
00:18:29,399 --> 00:18:30,720
the answer is valid.

303
00:18:30,767 --> 00:18:32,459
Do the warm-up tasks now.

304
00:18:32,479 --> 00:18:34,377
They take about ten minutes.

305
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:36,109
See you in Chapter 7.
